# {{title}} {#c-top}

An id names a block. A **coordinate** names something inside one — a cell, a
column, a reported row, a node in a value tree. It reads; it can never write.

=== table {#c-fy-facts caption="FY facts" format=csv header=1}
Segment, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4
Cloud,    8, 10, 12, 14
Platform, 5,  6,  7,  9
Services, 3,  4,  4,  5
===

=== view {#c-fy src=#c-fy-facts caption="FY with the full-year total" compute="FY [%.0f] = Q1 + Q2 + Q3 + Q4" summary="Segment = 'Total'; FY [%.0f] = sum(FY)"}
===

## Reading one cell into a sentence {#c-cells}

Cloud booked [[#c-fy[1]["Q1"]]] in Q1 and [[#c-fy[1]["FY"]]] across the year.
Platform's Q4 was [[#c-fy[2]["Q4"]]]. The company total is
[[#c-fy[summary]["FY"]]] — read out of the **reported row**, not retyped.

None of those numbers is written in this paragraph. Change a fact in
[[#c-fy-facts]] and every sentence above moves with it, including the total,
because the total is itself derived.

=== note {.tip #c-forms}
`#id[2]` is a body row · `#id[2]["Q1"]` one cell · `#id["Q1"]` a whole column ·
`#id[summary]["FY"]` the reported row · `other.geml#id[2]["Q1"]` the same in
another document. `[` cannot appear in a conforming id, so there is no ambiguity
about where the id ends and the coordinate begins.
===

## Walking a value tree {#c-tree}

=== data {#c-intake format=json}
{
  "sections": [
    { "name": "contact",
      "fields": [ { "name": "email", "required": true },
                  { "name": "phone", "required": false } ] },
    { "name": "billing",
      "fields": [ { "name": "vat", "required": false } ] }
  ]
}
===

The second field of the first section is called
[[#c-intake["sections"][0]["fields"][1]["name"]]], and the first section is
[[#c-intake["sections"][0]["name"]]]. A key is a quoted string; an index is a
bare integer.

## The document's own metadata {#c-meta}

`#meta` is a reserved id naming the merged meta namespace. This document is
version [[#meta["version"]]], kept by [[#meta["owner"]]] — read from the block
at the top of the file rather than repeated here.

=== note {#c-write .warning}
`geml get` and `geml set` both take a coordinate, so an agent can read one cell
without loading the table and write `#meta["version"]` without rewriting the
meta block. Writing goes to the **first** definition — the one that actually
wins when meta blocks merge.
===
